# Force LF line endings on text files regardless of host OS /
# git config, so source-lint tests that split on literal markers
# behave identically on Windows and Unix checkouts.
#
# Rationale: the workspace contains source-lint tests (in
# arxia-lattice/src/chain.rs, arxia-lattice/src/serialization.rs,
# examples/offline_payment/src/main.rs) that use
# `include_str!(...)` to read their own source at compile time
# and split on a literal byte sequence. Without this attribute
# file, Git for Windows applies `core.autocrlf=true` by default,
# converting LF→CRLF on checkout. The split markers are
# byte-literal LF; on a CRLF working tree the split fails, the
# tests fire spuriously, and `cargo test --workspace` exits 101
# on a fresh Windows clone — breaking the project's reproducible
# fresh-clone invariant for ~50% of contributing developers.
#
# This file is the root-cause fix. The source-lint tests have
# also been hardened in the same commit to be CRLF-tolerant
# (they now split on the bare `#[cfg(test)]` attribute), as
# defense-in-depth.
#
# Re-opens / contradicts Phase 1 findings MED-002 (commit 055)
# and MED-003 (commit 051): their source-lint test idioms were
# validated only on dev boxes preserving LF.

# Force LF on every Rust source, every TOML config, every
# Markdown doc, the workspace lockfile, every shell or CI
# script. These are all text-mode files where LF must be
# preserved across OSes.
*.rs        text eol=lf
*.toml      text eol=lf
*.md        text eol=lf
Cargo.lock  text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf
*.yaml      text eol=lf
*.sh        text eol=lf

# Windows-native scripts keep CRLF (PowerShell + .cmd convention).
*.ps1       text eol=crlf
*.cmd       text eol=crlf
*.bat       text eol=crlf

# Binary files: never normalised.
*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.ico       binary
*.crate     binary
